+
+## JSON Patch — `applyPatch(document, patch)`
+
+Applies `patch`, an array of operations, to `document` and returns the result.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { users: [{ name: 'Ada' }], active: true };
+
+applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: 'add', path: '/users/-', value: { name: 'Linus' } },
+  { op: 'copy', from: '/users/0/name', path: '/owner' },
+  { op: 'remove', path: '/active' },
+]);
+// { users: [{ name: 'Ada' }, { name: 'Linus' }], owner: 'Ada' }
+```
+
+Neither argument is modified: the result is a new value that shares no object or array with either of
+them, so it can be modified afterwards without reaching back into the document that was patched. The
+operations are applied in order, and the patch applies whole or not at all — when one operation
+fails, `applyPatch` throws and no partially patched document is returned.
+
+Each operation names its target with a JSON Pointer, read exactly as `resolvePointer` reads it,
+escaping and array-index rules included.
+
+| `op`        | Members          | Effect                                                                 |
+| ----------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `'add'`     | `path`, `value`  | Adds `value`: a member of an object, created or replaced; or an element inserted into an array, shifting the elements after it. |
+| `'remove'`  | `path`           | Removes the value, which must exist. An array closes up behind it.     |
+| `'replace'` | `path`, `value`  | Replaces the value, which must exist. It never creates one.            |
+| `'move'`    | `from`, `path`   | Removes the value at `from`, which must exist, then adds it at `path`.  |
+| `'copy'`    | `from`, `path`   | Adds a deep copy of the value at `from`, which must exist, at `path`.   |
+| `'test'`    | `path`, `value`  | Compares the value at `path`, which must exist, with `value`; changes nothing. |
+
+Members an operation does not define are ignored, as RFC 6902 section 4 requires, so an operation
+carrying a `"description"` of its own still applies.
+
+A few points that RFC 6902 settles and that are easy to get wrong:
+
+- `path` may be the empty pointer, the document itself: `add`, `replace` and `copy` then return a new
+  document, and `test` compares the whole of it. `remove` is the exception — removing the document
+  leaves no JSON value to return, so it is rejected as an invalid patch, as is a `move` out of it.
+- In `add`, and only in `add`, an array accepts the token `-`: the value is appended. An index is
+  accepted up to the array's length, where it also appends; beyond that it names no position.
+  Everywhere else `-` names no value, and an index must address an element the array holds.
+- `move` removes before it adds, so an array index in its `path` counts positions in the array the
+  removal leaves behind: moving `/0` to `/2` in `[1, 2, 3]` gives `[2, 3, 1]`.
+- `move` refuses to move a value into itself — a `from` that contains `path`, such as `/a` into
+  `/a/b`. `copy` has no such restriction: it copies the value before adding it.
+- `test` compares by value as JSON defines equality, so object members may come in any order, while
+  an array's order is significant. `1` and `1.0` are the same number; `1` and `'1'` are not.
+- An operation's `value` is copied into the result, so the patch can be applied twice, or kept, with
+  no risk of the document and the patch sharing a value.
+
+### Errors
+
+`applyPatch` throws a `JsonPatchError`, whose `code` says what went wrong, and whose `index` is the
+position in the patch of the operation that failed — `undefined` when the patch as a whole was
+rejected and no operation was reached.
+
+| `code`            | Cause                                                        | Examples                                        |
+| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |
+| `'invalid-patch'` | The patch is not an array of well-formed operations.         | a missing `path`, `op: 'update'`, a `value` on nothing but `add`/`replace`/`test`, `remove` of the whole document, `move` of a value into itself |
+| `'unresolved'`    | The patch is well-formed, but the document holds no value where the operation needs one. | `remove` of an absent member, `replace` of an out-of-range index, `add` under a parent that does not exist |
+| `'test-failed'`   | A `test` operation found a value other than the one it was given. | `test` of `1` against `'1'`                |
+
+`'unresolved'` means the same thing as it does on `JsonPointerError`, and covers a `test` whose
+target is absent: the comparison never happened, which is worth telling apart from a comparison that
+said no. A caller using `test` as a guard should treat both codes as the guard failing.
+
+When an operation fails because its pointer does not resolve, the `JsonPointerError` that says so
+precisely is kept as the `cause` of the `JsonPatchError`.
 
