+
+# Apply a patch to a deep copy, rather than sharing structure with the document
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` must return the patched document as a new value and must not modify its
+input. RFC 6902 section 5 adds that a patch applies whole or not at all: if one operation fails, the
+document must be left as it was. Two implementations satisfy both.
+
+The first is to copy the document once, then let the operations write into that copy in place. Cost:
+one traversal of the whole document per patch, whatever the patch does.
+
+The second is structural sharing — each operation rebuilds only the nodes on the path from the root to
+its target and reuses every other subtree, as persistent collections do. Cost: proportional to the
+depth of each pointer rather than to the size of the document, which is the shape dl-001 already
+preferred for resolution ("resolution proportional to the pointer's length instead of the document's
+size"). It is the faster option for the case this library is imported for: a small patch against a
+stored record or a configuration file.
+
+But sharing cuts both ways. Its result holds subtrees of the input document, so the two are entangled
+after the call: a caller that modifies the returned document — the ordinary thing to do with a value
+one has just been handed, and the reason it is returned as "a new value" — reaches into the document
+it patched. That is a defect that appears far from its cause, in a library used by several services,
+and no part of the published contract would warn about it. `test` makes it worse: a patch that only
+reads would still hand back a document aliasing the original.
+
+`copy` raises the same question inside one result. If the copied value were inserted by reference, two
+locations of the returned document would be the same object, and a later operation — or the caller —
+writing through one would be seen through the other.
+
+## Decision
+
+Copy the document once, with a `cloneJson` that recurses over arrays and own object members, and let
+the operations mutate that copy. The copy is the only thing they ever write to, so:
+
+- the document and the patch are never modified, whatever the operations do;
+- the result shares no object or array with either, and the caller may do as it likes with it;
+- atomicity is free: a failing operation throws, and the half-patched copy is dropped with it, since
+  nothing else ever held a reference to it.
+
+For the same reason, `copy` deep-copies the value it adds, and `add`, `replace` and `test` take their
+`value` from the patch through a copy as well, so that applying a patch twice cannot make two
+documents share a value with it. `move` is the one case that needs no copy: the value it adds has just
+been removed from the document, and nothing else holds it.
+
+Values are not deep-validated against `JsonValue` on the way in, consistently with dl-001: `cloneJson`
+copies arrays and objects and passes anything else through as a leaf.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A patch costs one traversal of the document, however small the patch. For the documents this library
+  is for — records, payloads, configuration — that is not worth trading the contract above for. If a
+  caller ever patches a document large enough for it to matter, the decision is worth revisiting, and
+  that caller's measurement should be the argument.
+- The result is a plain, fully independent JSON value. No caller needs to learn which parts of it are
+  safe to touch, and no caller has to copy it defensively.
+- `cloneJson` reads own members only, so a document is copied as data and carries nothing from a
+  prototype into its copy. Together with `setMember`, which defines properties instead of assigning
+  them, a patch that writes `/__proto__` stores a member of that name and does not change any object's
+  prototype.
+- The behaviour is observable, so it is in the README: the result shares nothing with the inputs. That
+  is now a promise, and moving to structural sharing later would break it.
