+
+# A JsonPatchError with three codes, reusing the pointer vocabulary
+
+## Context
+
+A patch can fail in ways that mean very different things to the caller, and dl-001 asked that later
+features reuse its error vocabulary — `'invalid-pointer'` and `'unresolved'` — instead of inventing
+their own. Those two codes do not cover JSON Patch on their own: a patch can be defective in ways that
+have nothing to do with a pointer (no `op`, no `value`, an operation that is not an object), and a
+`test` operation that says no is not a failure of the same kind at all — it is the operation working
+as intended, and a caller may well be using it as a guard.
+
+A patch is also a sequence. "The document holds no value there" is not actionable without knowing
+which operation said so, and reporting only a pointer would be ambiguous: the same pointer may appear
+in several operations.
+
+The last question was what to do with a `test` whose target does not exist. It could be folded into
+the failed comparison, which is what a caller guarding a document mostly wants to know, or reported as
+an unresolved location like every other operation's missing target.
+
+## Decision
+
+Throw a `JsonPatchError` for every failure, carrying:
+
+- `code`, one of `'invalid-patch'`, `'unresolved'` or `'test-failed'`;
+- `index`, the position in the patch of the operation that failed, or `undefined` when the patch as a
+  whole was rejected — it is not an array — and no operation was ever reached.
+
+`'unresolved'` is dl-001's code, with dl-001's meaning: the pointer is well-formed, the document holds
+no value at it. `'invalid-patch'` absorbs `'invalid-pointer'` rather than re-exporting it: a malformed
+pointer inside a patch is one way among many for the patch itself to be defective, and a caller that
+branches on it wants "this patch is a bug" either way. The pointer's own `JsonPointerError`, which
+says precisely what is malformed, is kept as the `cause`, and its `reason` is quoted in the message.
+
+Two judgements follow from the same principle — keep what the caller cannot reconstruct:
+
+- A `test` against a location that holds no value is `'unresolved'`, not `'test-failed'`. The
+  comparison never happened, and that is a different fact from a comparison that failed; a caller using
+  `test` as a guard treats both as the guard failing, which the README says.
+- Removing the whole document (`remove` with the empty pointer, or `move` with an empty `from`) is
+  `'invalid-patch'`, not `'unresolved'`. The root always resolves; the operation is the thing that
+  makes no sense, since there would be no JSON value left to return. The same goes for a `move` whose
+  `from` contains its `path`, which RFC 6902 section 4.4 forbids outright.
+
+Give `JsonPointerError` a `reason` field — the message without the pointer that prefixes it — so the
+patch module can quote it in a message that names its own operation and target. The alternative was
+nesting one full message inside another, which repeats the pointer twice in one line.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Three codes, and `index`, are now part of the published contract, next to the pointer's two. The
+  features that come after this one inherit both vocabularies and should keep extending them rather
+  than adding a third.
+- `'test-failed'` means exactly "a comparison said no", so a caller can log a stale document
+  differently from a missing one, and distinguish both from its own bug.
+- A caller that wants a single "the patch did not apply" signal catches `JsonPatchError` and ignores
+  `code`; nothing forces it to enumerate the three.
+- `JsonPointerError.reason` is public now, and duplicates part of `message`. It is additive, but it is
+  one more field to keep meaningful: it must stay the message's tail, with no pointer in it.
