+
+# A json-value module under the pointer and patch modules
+
+## Context
+
+dl-002 settled the shape of the package: one module per standard, `index.ts` as a re-export surface
+only, and internals shared between modules without being published. JSON Patch is the first module to
+test that arrangement, and it needs three things that are not about patching at all — a deep copy, the
+equality RFC 6902 section 4.6 defines, and a way to define an object member that does not trip over
+`__proto__`. It also needs two things that are about pointers and that `src/json-pointer.ts` kept to
+itself: the rule for reading a token as an array index, and a walk over tokens that have already been
+parsed, so that an operation can reach the parent of its target and the target itself from one parse.
+
+`JsonValue` and `JsonObject` lived in `src/json-pointer.ts`, because that was the only module when
+they were written. Leaving them there would make the patch module import the JSON data model from the
+module that resolves pointers, which is not where anyone would look for it, and would place the clone
+and equality functions either in the same wrong place or in the patch module, where a third feature
+would then have to import them from a standard it does not implement.
+
+## Decision
+
+Add `src/json-value.ts` under both standards: the JSON data model, `JsonValue` and `JsonObject`, and
+the three operations on it that the standards share — `setMember`, `cloneJson` and `equalJson`. It
+depends on nothing and knows nothing of pointers or patches. `index.ts` keeps publishing the two types
+from there, so the package's public surface does not change.
+
+Widen `src/json-pointer.ts`'s internal exports, as dl-002 anticipated, with `arrayIndex(token)` — the
+one rule that decides what indexes an array, now used by `member` and by the patch operations — and
+`resolveTokens(document, tokens, pointer)`, the walk `resolvePointer` is reduced to.
+
+`src/json-patch.ts` then holds nothing but RFC 6902: the operation types, the error, the six
+operations, and the reading of an operation object as it arrives from a payload.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The layering is explicit and one-directional: `json-value` ← `json-pointer` ← `json-patch`, with
+  `index.ts` above all three. The next standard picks the layer it belongs to instead of growing one
+  of the existing modules.
+- `cloneJson` and `equalJson` are internal, so they can be adjusted with the features that use them.
+  `equalJson` is written to RFC 6902's definition of equality; if another standard needs a different
+  one, it brings its own rather than bending this one.
+- `arrayIndex` and `resolveTokens` are internal too, but they are now the single definition of "this
+  token indexes an array" and "walk these tokens" for the whole package. An operation cannot drift
+  from resolution by re-implementing either.
+- `resolveTokens` takes the pointer the tokens came from only to name it in an error. The patch module
+  passes the whole pointer while walking a prefix of it on purpose, so that a failure is reported
+  against the target the operation wrote rather than against a prefix the patch never mentioned.
+- One line of existing code changed beyond the moves: `member` now uses `Object.hasOwn` rather than
+  `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call`, so that both modules read the same way.
