+
+# Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch
+
+## Request
+
+Let the services change JSON documents with standard patches: implement JSON Patch per RFC 6902, with
+its six operations — `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` — exporting
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies an array of operations to a document and
+returns the result as a new value, without modifying its input, and throws when the patch is invalid
+or one of its operations fails.
+
+## What was done
+
+Tests first, as the testing directive requires. Every acceptance criterion was new behaviour, so all
+of it was red-first: `test/json-patch.test.ts` was written and run before `src/json-patch.ts` existed,
+and failed on the missing export.
+
+- `src/json-value.ts` — new, under both standards (dl-005): the JSON data model, `JsonValue` and
+  `JsonObject`, moved here from the pointer module, plus `setMember`, `cloneJson` and `equalJson`.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — two internals exported for the operations that write through a pointer, as
+  dl-002 anticipated: `arrayIndex`, now the single rule for reading a token as an array index, and
+  `resolveTokens`, the walk `resolvePointer` is now reduced to. `JsonPointerError` gained a `reason`
+  field (dl-004), so a patch can quote it in a message of its own.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — the operation types, `JsonPatchError`, `applyPatch`, the six operations, and
+  the reading of an operation object as it arrives from a payload.
+- `src/index.ts` — adds `applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`, `JsonPatch`, `JsonPatchOperation`,
+  `JsonPatchErrorCode` and the six operation interfaces. The types keep being published from where
+  they now live; nothing that was public has changed.
+- `test/json-patch.test.ts` — 39 `node:test` tests.
+- `README.md` — a section on `applyPatch`: the six operations, what the standard settles and is easy
+  to get wrong, the three error codes. The JSON Pointer section's note on `-` now points to the
+  `add` operation that gives it a meaning.
+- `.wingfoil/dna.yaml` — the `json-patch` and `json-value` modules, via `wingfoil dna add`.
+
+## Coverage
+
+The project declares no coverage threshold, so the bar is the testing directive's: a happy path and an
+edge or error path for each behaviour.
+
+- Conformance: the sixteen examples of RFC 6902 appendix A that a library can run (A.13 is a
+  JSON-parsing concern, duplicate keys in one operation object).
+- Each operation: its happy path on an object, on an array and on the whole document; and its failures
+  — an absent target, an index out of range, `-` where no value is stored, a parent that does not exist
+  or holds no members, an array token that is not an index.
+- The order of operations: a patch whose operations build on each other; `move` removing before it adds,
+  so an array index in its `path` counts positions in the shortened array.
+- What RFC 6902 forbids: `remove` of the whole document, `move` of a value into itself, and the
+  near-misses that are allowed — a location onto itself, and a sibling whose name shares its prefix.
+- `test`: equality across every JSON type, member order being insignificant, `1` and `1.0` being the
+  same number, and the look-alikes that must fail (`'1'`, `true`, `null`, a reordered array, an extra
+  member). An absent target is reported as `unresolved`, not as a failed comparison.
+- Isolation: the document is untouched; the patch is untouched and shares no value with the result; the
+  result can be modified without reaching into the document; a patch that fails part-way through leaves
+  the document as it was.
+- Validation: a patch that is not an array, an operation that is not an object, an `op` outside the six,
+  a `path` or `from` missing or malformed, a `value` missing or `undefined`, and members an operation
+  does not define being ignored.
+- `add` of `/__proto__` stores a member of that name and leaves every prototype alone.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 59 tests, 59 pass, 0 fail: the 20 of the pointer suite, unchanged, and 39 new ones.
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, `strict`, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, with the `.ts` import specifiers rewritten to `.js`.
+- A consumer script run against `dist/` applies a patch, catches a `JsonPatchError` with its `code` and
+  `index`, and confirms the document it passed in is unchanged.
+- Error messages were read by hand, one per failure kind: each names the operation's position, its `op`
+  and its target, e.g. `JSON Patch operation 0: "remove" "/nope": "nope" is not a member of the
+  object`. Where a pointer is what failed, the `JsonPointerError` is kept as the `cause`.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-003` — applying a patch to a deep copy, rather than sharing structure with the document.
+- `dl-004` — a `JsonPatchError` with three codes, reusing the pointer vocabulary.
+- `dl-005` — a `json-value` module under the pointer and patch modules.
+
+## For the approver
+
+The three decision-logs are submitted and await approval. Two are worth a look before the next feature
+is built on them:
+
+- `dl-003` chooses a whole-document copy per patch over structural sharing, trading an O(document)
+  traversal for a result that shares nothing with its inputs. That trade is now a promise in the README.
+- `dl-004` extends the published error vocabulary: `'invalid-patch'`, `'unresolved'` and
+  `'test-failed'`, plus the `index` of the failing operation, and adds `reason` to `JsonPointerError`.
+  A `test` against a location that holds no value reports `'unresolved'` rather than `'test-failed'`;
+  the README says a caller guarding with `test` should treat both as the guard failing.
